; ======================================================================
; pi4BootContainerSelfTest.pi4 - the executable gate for the BOOTLOADER:
; Anvil/Core/pmfboot.pbi's verify-place-enter path.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; This is the one part of Anvil that must never be wrong, because being
; wrong means entering memory that is not the program somebody asked for.
; Loud errors beat silent wrong answers, and that rule has no sharper case
; anywhere in this program.
;
; So this file proves the three outcomes that matter, over the REAL code
; path rather than a re-implementation of it:
;
;   (a) A GOOD CONTAINER IS ENTERED, and the payload demonstrably ran.
;       Not "the command returned 1" - the payload writes a known word to
;       a known address and returns a known value in x0, and both are read
;       back out of memory afterwards. A pass that only checks a return
;       code cannot tell "entered it" from "decided it would have".
;
;   (b) A CONTAINER WITH ONE CORRUPTED BYTE IS REFUSED by the hash check,
;       and the payload is NOT entered. The corruption is one bit in the
;       middle of the image - the kind a bad sector or a truncated copy
;       actually produces, not a mangled header that a dozen earlier
;       checks would catch first. The point is that the LAST guard holds
;       when every earlier one has passed.
;
;   (c) A CONTAINER WHOSE LOAD ADDRESS OVERLAPS THE MONITOR IS REFUSED,
;       before a single byte is written anywhere.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; HOW IT RUNS, AND WHY IT IS AN INSTRUMENT AND NOT AN EXAMPLE
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; With no Pi 4 attached, the vehicle is the project's own A64 oracle. A
; gate runs on the board when a board is attached; a no-board run is what
; the emulator stays right for, and this is one. The harness is
; tools/a64/a64_bootcontainer_check.py, which builds this file, stages
; three containers into memory, runs each one, and reads the verdict out.
;
; It also prints its verdict over the UART, so the identical binary is the
; stronger proof the day a Pi 4 is attached: receive it, run it, read the
; tally. Nothing here needs a display, a mailbox, a card or a network.
;
; DIAGNOSTIC. Does not ship.
;
;   PureMetalForge.exe --compile RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/pi4BootContainerSelfTest.pi4
;        -t pi4 --load-addr 0x400000 --stack-addr 0x3000000 --entry-returns
;        -o _work/bootc.img
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHY THE HARNESS BUILDS THE CONTAINERS AND THIS FILE DOES NOT
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; It would have been easier to have this program construct its own header
; and its own payload in memory and then boot it. That is a WEAKER test
; and the weakness is the interesting kind: a header written by the same
; source file that reads it agrees with itself no matter what the layout
; actually is. Get the offset of `load` wrong in both places and the test
; passes while every real container fails.
;
; So the containers come from OUTSIDE, byte by byte, from a Python harness
; that lays the header out from the table in Anvil/Core/pmfboot.pbi's own
; documentation and computes the digest with hashlib. Three independent
; statements of the format - the compiler's writer, this monitor's reader,
; and the harness - and the test only passes when all three agree.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE PAYLOAD, AND WHY IT LOOKS LIKE THAT
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Four A64 instructions, assembled by the harness and placed as the
; container's image:
;
;     movz x1, #0xBEEF               a value nothing else would produce
;     movz x2, #0x0130, lsl #16      the observation address, $01300000
;     str  x1, [x2]                  the known write
;     movz x0, #0x5A5A               the known return value
;     ret                            back to the monitor
;
; It touches nothing else, needs no stack beyond the one it is handed, and
; is position-independent by construction - no ADRP, no literal pool, no
; branch. That last property matters: the whole point of the container is
; that the monitor PLACES the image at an address, so a payload that only
; worked at one address would not be testing placement.
;
; $01300000 for the observation and $01200000 for the load are both inside
; the low payload window (up to $07EFFFFF; its base is above the monitor
; and `map` prints it), well clear of this program's own image wherever
; it was staged, and inside the oracle's flat memory.
; ======================================================================

XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4"
; timer.pi4 is INCLUDED but TimerInit() is never CALLED - see Main. It is
; here because Anvil/Core/rxbreak.pbi names Ticks() inside RxByte, the
; transfer protocol's timeout read, which this program never executes.
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/timer.pi4"
; safety.pi4 BEFORE memmap.pi4, which is where board.pi4 puts it and for
; the same reason: memmap.pi4's boot-trail writer pets the PM watchdog
; when the trail armed one (MonTrailAppend, MonTrailEnd, MonTrailDogArm),
; so the memory map does not compile on its own. This program arms
; nothing and calls none of it - the trail's own "is it on" word is zero
; in this program's memory - but a name a file CALLS has to exist, and
; the answer to that is the real library in the order the monitor uses,
; not a procedure of this program's own pretending to be it.
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/safety.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Board/memmap.pi4"

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The capability declarations this instrument needs. It builds none of
;  the gated command families; #CAP_STORAGE is here because
;  Anvil/Core/pmfboot.pbi names it in CmdBoot's gate, and CmdBoot is
;  compiled even though this program never calls it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#CAP_STORAGE  = 1
#CAP_NET      = 0
#CAP_GPIO     = 0
#CAP_I2C      = 0
#CAP_MMC      = 0
#CAP_USB      = 0
#CAP_BOOT_EL1 = 0

XIncludeFile "Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi"

; ======================================================================
;  THE STORAGE SEAM, STUBBED - and stubbed HONESTLY, not plausibly.
;
;  This instrument proves the boot-from-MEMORY path, which touches no
;  medium at all. But Anvil/Core/pmfboot.pbi is compiled whole, and its
;  file path names HwFileOpen and the rest, so the names must exist.
;
;  EVERY ONE OF THEM REFUSES. Not "returns something harmless" - refuses,
;  with #HW_FILE_NOMEDIUM, which is the truth: this program has no medium.
;  A stub that answered a read with zeroes would let a future edit route
;  the tested path through the file reader by mistake and still go green.
; ======================================================================
Global gHwFileErr.i

Procedure.i HwFileLastError()
  ProcedureReturn gHwFileErr
EndProcedure
Procedure.i HwStorageUp()
  gHwFileErr = #HW_FILE_NOMEDIUM
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
Procedure.i HwFileOpen(*name)
  gHwFileErr = #HW_FILE_NOMEDIUM
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
Procedure.i HwFileSize()
  gHwFileErr = #HW_FILE_NOMEDIUM
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
Procedure.i HwFileReadAt(off.i, dst.i, n.i)
  gHwFileErr = #HW_FILE_NOMEDIUM
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure
Procedure HwFileClose()
EndProcedure
Procedure.i HwFileWritable()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
Procedure.i HwFileWriteAll(*name, src.i, n.i)
  gHwFileErr = #HW_FILE_NOMEDIUM
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
Procedure.i HwFileErrorText()
  ProcedureReturn "this diagnostic has no storage medium at all - it proves the boot-from-memory path"
EndProcedure
Procedure.i HwFileCanList()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
Procedure HwStorageReport()
  PrintN("no medium: this is a diagnostic, not the monitor.")
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  NO SETTINGS STORE AND NO LINE EDITOR, and their absence is the reason
;  Anvil/Core/pmfboot.pbi is a separate file from
;  Anvil/Core/bootfile_cmd.pbi.
;
;  The `boot` command word reads a command line, and Anvil/Core/parse.pbi
;  reaches for a USB keyboard, a mouse tick and a Wi-Fi console to do it.
;  The four boot.* settings need a 2,600-line KEY=VALUE store on a medium
;  this program does not have. Neither has anything whatever to do with
;  whether a hash is checked before a jump - and faking both, just to ask
;  that question, is how a gate ends up testing its own scaffolding.
;
;  So the container ENGINE builds on its own, and this file builds only
;  that. What it includes below is the engine's entire dependency list.
; ======================================================================

; ======================================================================
;  THE REST OF THE SEAM THE BOOT ENGINE NAMES ON ANVIL MAIN, STUBBED.
;
;  Anvil/Core/rxbreak.pbi reads its cancellation byte from EITHER console -
;  the UART or the network console - so it names three NetConsole*
;  procedures. This instrument has no network: the pump does nothing and
;  the network source never has a byte (-1), which is the truth, and the
;  UART path the harness models is untouched.
;
;  Anvil/Core/pmfboot.pbi calls ScreenServiceTick() between long phases so a
;  board with a display can repaint during a large boot. There is no
;  display here, so it does nothing.
;
;  HwPmfTargetId() is what a version-2 container's target field is judged
;  against. The containers the harness stages for cases 1 to 5 are version
;  1, which carry no target and never ask; the answer is the Pi 4's
;  compiler target id (RaspberryPi4/Board/hw_id.pi4) so that a version-2
;  container staged here is judged the way the board would judge it.
; ======================================================================
Procedure NetConsoleClaimLocal()
EndProcedure
Procedure NetConsolePump()
EndProcedure
Procedure.i NetConsoleGetc()
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure
Procedure ScreenServiceTick()
EndProcedure
Procedure.i HwPmfTargetId()
  ProcedureReturn 2711
EndProcedure

XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/state.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/format.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/rxbreak.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/memrange.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/sha256.pbi"

; ======================================================================
;  RunAt(a) - THE JUMP, and this is the board seam this instrument has to
;  supply for itself.
;
;  On the Pi 4 RunAt lives in RaspberryPi4/Board/cache.pi4 and does three
;  board things around the call: it stops the ethernet DMA, it flushes and
;  disables the caches, and it arms the deadman. None of those exist here
;  and none of them is what is under test. What IS under test is that
;  pmfboot.pi4 reaches this call, with the right address, only when every
;  guard has passed - so this is the call and nothing else.
;
;  THE INDIRECT CALL IS ASSEMBLY AND HAS TO BE. The project prefers
;  libraries to intrinsics unless the language genuinely cannot express
;  something, and a computed jump is the first thing on that list. This language has no way to spell a call through a
;  variable, which is why cache.pi4's CallAddr is written exactly this way
;  and why the same shape is repeated here rather than invented.
;
;  "ldr x9", not "ldr w9": an ldr w9 would branch to the low half of the
;  address, which for anything above 4 GB is a wild branch into
;  plausible-looking low memory. cache.pi4 records the same warning.
; ======================================================================
Procedure BootCall()
  ASM
    adrp x9, global_ggoaddr
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_ggoaddr
    ldr  x9, [x9]
    blr  x9
    adrp x10, global_ggorc
    add  x10, x10, #:lo12:global_ggorc
    str  x0, [x10]
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i RunAt(a.i)
  ; The one address it will not jump to is the monitor's own - the same
  ; refusal cache.pi4's RunAt makes, kept because pmfboot.pi4 is entitled
  ; to assume the seam has it.
  If HitsMonitor(a, a) <> 0
    Print("!! ")
    PutAddr(a)
    PrintN(" is inside the monitor itself, so nothing was run.")
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  gGoAddr = a
  gGoRc = 0
  BootCall()
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE PAYLOAD ABI, STUBBED - and stubbed so that reaching it is LOUD.
;
;  Anvil/Core/pmfboot.pbi's PmfEnter() gained three references when the
;  payload ABI landed on 2026-09-04: it sets gSvcMayReturn from the
;  container's flags word, and - only for a container that declared
;  #PMF_FLAG_WANTS_SERVICES - calls BuildServiceTable() and
;  SvcTableAddr(). This instrument builds pmfboot.pi4 WHOLE, so the three
;  names must exist here even though no case reaches them:
;
;    * cases 2, 3, 4 and 5 are refused before PmfEnter is called at all;
;    * case 1's container declares bit 0 only, so the flag branch is not
;      taken.
;
;  IT DOES NOT INCLUDE Anvil/Hal/abi.pbi, and that is the same judgement
;  the HwFile* stubs above are built on. abi.pi4 rides the whole Hw* seam -
;  storage, settings, GPIO, I2C, USB, the console - and pulling it in would
;  mean stubbing all of that to prove something about a hash. One include
;  boundary in the right place is the difference between a testable
;  bootloader and one that is only ever exercised by hand on a board, and
;  this file's footer already says so.
;
;  SvcTableAddr RETURNS 0 AND BuildServiceTable SHOUTS. Not "returns
;  something harmless" - a stub that handed back a plausible address would
;  let a future edit route a tested path through a table that does not
;  exist and still go green. If either is ever reached here, the transcript
;  says which and the run is a fault in the harness, not a pass.
; ======================================================================
Global gSvcMayReturn.i

Procedure BuildServiceTable()
  PrintN("!! this instrument has no service table, and PmfEnter just asked for one.")
  PrintN("   No case here declares the wants-services flag, so reaching this line")
  PrintN("   means a container was staged with bit 2 set and was NOT refused by the")
  PrintN("   ninth guard - which is case 5's whole subject. Treat this run as a")
  PrintN("   fault in the harness and not as a result.")
EndProcedure

Procedure.i SvcTableAddr()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; The boot code compares a container header against the payload ABI
; version. That number lives in its own file so this probe, which builds a
; monitor with NO service table on purpose, reads the same one the monitor
; does instead of carrying a copy that drifts.
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Hal/abi_version.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/pmfboot.pbi"

; ======================================================================
;  WHERE THE HARNESS PUTS THINGS. These four addresses are the contract
;  between this file and tools/a64/a64_bootcontainer_check.py, and they
;  are stated in both. All four are inside the low payload window (up to
;  $07EFFFFF; its base is above the monitor and `map` prints it) and clear
;  of this program's own image, which is well under a megabyte wherever
;  the board staged it.
; ======================================================================
#BC_CASECELL  = $01100000      ; where the harness stages the CASE NUMBER
#BC_CONTAINER = $01000000      ; where the harness stages the container
#BC_LOAD      = $01200000      ; where its header says the image goes
#BC_OBSERVE   = $01300000      ; where the payload writes its known word
#BC_MAGICVAL  = $BEEF          ; ... and what it writes
#BC_RETVAL    = $5A5A          ; what the payload returns in x0

; ======================================================================
;  THE VERDICT, in globals the harness reads out of the .sym map.
;
;  IT IS ALSO PRINTED. A verdict that lives only in memory is the reason: a tally readable only from a dump is what forced
;  gates into the emulator in the first place. On a board, receive this
;  and run it and the UART says the same thing.
; ======================================================================
Global gBcCase.i               ; which case the harness asked for
Global gBcBooted.i             ; 1 if PmfBootAt reported it entered
Global gBcObserved.i           ; what is at #BC_OBSERVE afterwards
Global gBcRc.i                 ; x0 the payload returned
Global gBcPass.i               ; 1 = this case behaved as it must
Global gBcRan.i                ; 1 = Main reached the end. Guards a dump
                               ; read from a program that faulted early.
Global gBcPlaced.i             ; the first word at #BC_LOAD afterwards.
                               ; CASE 5's WHOLE POINT: the ninth guard runs
                               ; before a byte is copied, so "refused" is
                               ; not enough - it must be refused HAVING
                               ; DONE NOTHING, and the only way to say that
                               ; out loud is to prove the load address was
                               ; never written.

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE CASE NUMBER ARRIVES IN MEMORY, NOT IN A GLOBAL, and the first
;  attempt at this got it wrong in a way worth recording.
;
;  It was a `Global gBcWant.i = -1` that the harness poked before starting
;  the program. Every case then ran as -1. The reason is _start: a compiled
;  image initialises its data and clears its BSS in its first
;  instructions, so ANY global the harness writes before execution is
;  overwritten before Main() ever looks at it. That is correct behaviour
;  from the image and a wrong assumption in the harness.
;
;  So the case number lives at a fixed address in the payload window,
;  which _start has no reason to touch and does not. #BC_CASECELL is that
;  address, and it is stated in this file and in the harness both.
;
;  It reads as 0 - "no case staged" - if the poke never happened, which
;  Main below reports as a fault in the harness rather than treating as a
;  pass.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

Procedure PutCaseName(c.i)
  If c = 1
    Print("a good container")
  ElseIf c = 2
    Print("a container with one corrupted image byte")
  ElseIf c = 3
    Print("a container whose load address overlaps the monitor")
  ElseIf c = 4
    Print("a container whose entry is outside its own image")
  ElseIf c = 5
    Print("a container asking for BOTH a device tree and the service table")
  Else
    Print("an unknown case")
  EndIf
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Main()
  Define booted.i

  UartInit(115200)
  ; NO TimerInit(). It reads CNTFRQ_EL0 with an MRS, and the A64 oracle
  ; models neither system register - correctly, since it is an
  ; INTERPRETER of the integer subset the backend emits and not a model of
  ; the BCM2711's timer block. Nothing on the path under test needs a
  ; clock: OutBreak() polls UartReadReady() and does not time anything,
  ; and the countdown that does live in Anvil/Core/bootfile_cmd.pbi, which
  ; this instrument deliberately does not build. On a board the call would
  ; be free and harmless; leaving it out is what makes the identical
  ; source run in both places.
  gBad = 0
  gFail = 0
  gHaveEntry = 0

  gBcCase = PeekI(#BC_CASECELL)
  gBcPass = 0
  gBcRan  = 0

  PrintN("pi4BootContainerSelfTest - the bootloader's verify-place-enter path.")
  Print("Case ")
  PrintDec(gBcCase)
  Print(": ")
  PutCaseName(gBcCase)
  PrintN(".")
  PrintNl()

  ; 1..5. Case 4 (entry outside the image) and case 5 (both x0 flags) are
  ; staged by the harness; 4 only under --mutate, 5 in every run - the
  ; ninth guard landed with the payload ABI on 2026-09-04 and is a
  ; standing case, not a mutant control.
  If gBcCase < 1 Or gBcCase > 5
    PrintN("!! no case was staged. The harness pokes a case number into gBcWant")
    PrintN("   before the run, and it did not, so nothing was tested. This is a")
    PrintN("   fault in the harness and NOT a pass.")
    gBcRan = 1
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ; The observation cell is cleared FIRST, so that "the payload wrote it"
  ; cannot be confused with "it happened to be there". On the oracle
  ; memory reads as 0 anyway; on a board it reads as whatever was there.
  PokeI(#BC_OBSERVE, 0)
  ; AND THE LOAD ADDRESS, for the same reason and one step further. Case 5
  ; must be refused before a byte is placed, so this cell has to start at
  ; a known value for "it was never written" to mean anything.
  PokeI(#BC_LOAD, 0)

  booted = PmfBootAt(#BC_CONTAINER)

  gBcBooted   = booted
  gBcObserved = PeekI(#BC_OBSERVE)
  gBcPlaced   = PeekI(#BC_LOAD)
  gBcRc       = gGoRc

  PrintNl()
  PrintN("---- what happened ----")
  Print("  PmfBootAt returned   ")
  PrintDec(booted)
  PrintNl()
  Print("  word at ")
  PutAddr(#BC_OBSERVE)
  Print("     ")
  PutHex16(gBcObserved)
  PrintNl()
  Print("  payload x0           ")
  PutHex16(gBcRc)
  PrintNl()
  Print("  word at ")
  PutAddr(#BC_LOAD)
  Print("     ")
  PutHex16(gBcPlaced)
  PrintNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  THE VERDICT PER CASE. Each states what MUST be true, and each
  ;  requires the negative as well as the positive: case 1 is not a pass
  ;  merely because it returned 1, and cases 2 and 3 are not passes
  ;  merely because they returned 0 - the payload must be proved NOT to
  ;  have run, or a refusal that happened after the jump would look
  ;  exactly like a refusal that happened before it.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  If gBcCase = 1
    If booted <> 0 And gBcObserved = #BC_MAGICVAL And gBcRc = #BC_RETVAL
      gBcPass = 1
      PrintN("PASS: the container was verified, placed and ENTERED, and the payload")
      PrintN("      demonstrably ran - it wrote its word and returned its value.")
    Else
      PrintN("FAIL: a good container was not entered, or the payload did not run.")
      If booted = 0
        PrintN("      PmfBootAt refused it. The lines above say why.")
      EndIf
      If gBcObserved <> #BC_MAGICVAL
        PrintN("      The known write is not at the observation address, so the")
        PrintN("      payload never executed even if the command claimed it did.")
      EndIf
      If gBcRc <> #BC_RETVAL
        PrintN("      x0 is not the payload's return value.")
      EndIf
    EndIf

  ElseIf gBcCase = 2
    If booted = 0 And gBcObserved = 0 And gBcRc = 0
      gBcPass = 1
      PrintN("PASS: the corrupted container was REFUSED by the hash check, and the")
      PrintN("      payload was not entered - the observation cell is untouched.")
    Else
      PrintN("FAIL: a container with a corrupted image byte was not refused, or was")
      PrintN("      refused only after the payload had already been entered.")
      If booted <> 0
        PrintN("      PmfBootAt reported success. THE HASH CHECK DID NOT HOLD. This is")
        PrintN("      the worst possible outcome for this program: the last guard")
        PrintN("      before the jump let a corrupted image through.")
      EndIf
      If gBcObserved <> 0
        PrintN("      The payload RAN. Whatever printed above, the jump happened.")
      EndIf
    EndIf

  ElseIf gBcCase = 5
    ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
    ;  THE NINTH GUARD. Bit 1 says "put a device tree in x0" and bit 2
    ;  says "put the service table in x0", and there is one x0.
    ;
    ;  THIS CASE IS STRICTER THAN 2 AND 3, and deliberately: those two are
    ;  refused AFTER the image has been placed (the hash cannot run before
    ;  placement, and the range check is about where placement would go).
    ;  The ninth guard runs before the image length is even checked, so
    ;  the container must be refused HAVING DONE NOTHING - and gBcPlaced
    ;  is what says so. A guard that refused correctly but only after
    ;  copying a megabyte over the operator's memory would pass every
    ;  other check here.
    ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
    If booted = 0 And gBcObserved = 0 And gBcRc = 0 And gBcPlaced = 0
      gBcPass = 1
      PrintN("PASS: the container asking for both a device tree and the service")
      PrintN("      table in x0 was REFUSED, and nothing was placed, entered or")
      PrintN("      written - the load address still holds what it held before.")
    Else
      PrintN("FAIL: a container with bits 1 and 2 both set was not refused before")
      PrintN("      anything happened.")
      If booted <> 0
        PrintN("      PmfBootAt reported success. It was ENTERED with one of two")
        PrintN("      pointers it asked for, and which one is a coin toss.")
      EndIf
      If gBcPlaced <> 0
        PrintN("      The image was PLACED. The refusal came too late: this guard")
        PrintN("      runs before the image length is checked precisely so that a")
        PrintN("      container this confused never touches memory at all.")
      EndIf
    EndIf

  Else
    If booted = 0 And gBcObserved = 0 And gBcRc = 0
      gBcPass = 1
      PrintN("PASS: the container whose load address overlaps the monitor was")
      PrintN("      REFUSED, and nothing was placed or entered.")
    Else
      PrintN("FAIL: a container asking to be loaded over the monitor was not")
      PrintN("      refused. On a board that would have overwritten the code that")
      PrintN("      was reading it, with no prompt left to say so.")
    EndIf
  EndIf

  PrintNl()
  If gBcPass <> 0
    PrintN("RESULT: GREEN")
  Else
    PrintN("RESULT: RED")
  EndIf

  gBcRan = 1
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Main()
